<section class="reference-section" id="great-rishis"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Great Rishis</h2><a href="/great-rishis.html">Return to the profiles</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>These original profiles use the owner-supplied Bhagavata Volume 1, Griffith Ramayana PDF and English Skanda Purana Part 5. Chapter/canto references are primary. Bhagavata and Griffith PDF positions below identify the supplied reflowed files, not original printed pagination. The Skanda scan retains printed page numbers. Private PDFs and review images are not distributed.</p><section id="rishi-narada"><h3>Narada and Dhruva</h3><p><em>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran</em>, Gita Press, Volume 1 (Skandhas 1–8), <a href="https://archive.org/details/srimad-bhagavat-mahapuran-2-volume-set-sanskrit-hindi/Srimad%20Bhagavat%20Mahapuran%20Volume%201%20Sanskrit%20Hindi/mode/2up" target="_blank" rel="noopener">exact supplied edition</a>: <strong>4.8.25–44, 53–63, 68–70</strong>, supplied reflow-file pp. 734–741. Narada’s initial counsel, Dhruva’s response, subsequent guidance and reassurance to Uttanapada support the profile. <strong>4.8.38</strong> includes Dhruva’s description of the travelling sage with a vina. The full Dhruva reader follows these chapters.</p></section><section id="rishi-vashishtha"><h3>Vashishtha in the Ramayana</h3><p>Ralph T. H. Griffith, <em>The Ramayan of Valmiki</em>, owner-supplied English PDF: <strong>Book I, Canto VII</strong>, supplied-file p. 87 (royal priest and counsellor); <strong>Book II, Cantos III and V</strong>, pp. 470–471, 485–486 (consecration preparations and the visit to Rama and Sita); <strong>II.XXXVII</strong>, pp. 700–702 (the bark garments and Vashishtha’s protest to Kaikeyi). The file has 2,732 reflowed pages. The link to Rama’s exile supplies the wider departure setting; the shorter reader does not name every figure in the fuller account.</p></section><section id="rishi-atri"><h3>Atri and Anasuya in two accounts</h3><p>Bhagavata <strong>4.1.15–33</strong>, supplied reflow-file pp. 671–673: Atri and Anasuya, the tapas at Rksha/Kula mountain, the appearance of Brahma, Vishnu and Shiva, and the sons’ associations. In this passage Soma is associated with Brahma, Dattatreya with Vishnu and Durvasa with Shiva. These relationships are retained as this account presents them.</p><p>Griffith Ramayana <strong>II.CXVII</strong>, supplied-file pp. 1133–1135, gives Atri’s welcome to Rama, Sita and Lakshmana and introduces Anasuya. <strong>II.CXVIII</strong>, pp. 1138–1141, gives Anasuya’s conversation and gifts; <strong>II.CXIX</strong> begins on p. 1146. Anasuya’s own actions are not attributed to Atri. This visit follows Chitrakuta, beyond the endpoint of the existing condensed exile reader.</p></section><section id="rishi-bhrigu"><h3>Bhrigu: genealogy, conflict and prayer</h3><p>Bhagavata <strong>4.1.43–45</strong>, reflow-file pp. 675–676, supplies Bhrigu and Khyati’s family and the line <strong>Bhrigu/Khyati → Dhata/Ayati → Mrikanda → Markandeya</strong>. Bhrigu is an ancestor rather than Markandeya’s immediate father. <strong>4.2.27–32</strong>, pp. 683–684, describes the countercurse in the Daksha/Nandi conflict. <strong>4.7.30</strong>, pp. 720–721, gives Bhrigu’s prayer acknowledging obscured self-knowledge and seeking grace. The profile preserves these as episodes in the Bhagavata’s Daksha-sacrifice account.</p></section><section id="rishi-markandeya"><h3>Markandeya’s cosmic vision</h3><p><em>Skanda Purana</em>, Part 5, Motilal Banarsidass supplied scan, Vaishnava-khanda, Purushottama-ksetra-mahatmya, <strong>II.ii.3</strong>, printed pp. 9–14; supplied PDF pp. 13–18. Verses <strong>2–16</strong> establish the dissolution waters, the banyan and divine encounter; <strong>17–26</strong> give the sage’s prayer; <strong>27–39</strong> describe the child and Markandeya’s vision of fourteen worlds and the contents of the cosmic egg; <strong>40–48</strong> explain the wonder within Purushottama’s sacred setting; <strong>49–51</strong> describe Maheshvara worship and conquest of Death. No translator/editor attribution is inferred from a filename.</p><p>The Lokas guide uses a Bhagavata enumeration, while this vision uses the Skanda passage. The chapter’s own lower-world footnote has a different enumeration; its list is not silently replaced by the other book’s. The profile therefore refers to fourteen worlds without combining the two arrangements into a single chart.</p></section><section id="rishi-connections"><h3>How the links are used</h3><p>Bhagavata <strong>3.12.21–24</strong>, supplied reflow-file pp. 486–487, names Atri, Bhrigu, Vashishtha and Narada among Brahma’s sons in that creation account. This supports the shared creation/lineage context.</p><p>Direct links to Narada in Dhruva, Shiva and Parvati, and Lalita and Bhandasura refer to his role already present in those existing readers, each of which retains its own textual references. Vashishtha and Atri link to the wider Ramayana journey. Bhrigu and Markandeya have a family connection in the Bhagavata. The Markandeya links to the creation and Lokas guides are related cosmological readings, not a claim that every page retells his vision.</p></section><p><strong>Illustrations:</strong> two newly AI-generated conceptual scenes. The hermitage is imagined. The teaching gathering shows unnamed figures rather than portraits of the five sages or a reconstruction of one textual episode. Each illustration appears once.</p></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="creation-universe"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>The Creation of the Universe</h2><a href="/creation-universe.html">Return to the page</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p id="ref-creation-universe">The explicit Sarga–Visarga distinction follows the owner-supplied Bhagavata edition. Brahmanda and Linga passages are identified as comparisons; their classifications are not silently merged into the Bhagavata’s terminology. The article is an original English explanation, not a direct translation. Illustrations are contemporary conceptual interpretations.</p><section id="creation-bhagavata"><h3>Bhagavata Purana: Sarga and Visarga</h3><p><em>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran, Volume 1, Sanskrit–Hindi</em>, Gita Press, Gorakhpur, from the owner-supplied <a href="https://archive.org/details/srimad-bhagavat-mahapuran-2-volume-set-sanskrit-hindi/Srimad%20Bhagavat%20Mahapuran%20Volume%201%20Sanskrit%20Hindi/mode/2up" rel="noopener" target="_blank">first-volume link</a>. Both definitions occur in <strong>2.10.3</strong>; 2.10.1–2 places them among the text’s subjects. Further passages: <strong>3.5.26–35</strong> (mahat, ahamkara and cosmic constituents), <strong>3.6.1–6</strong> (unification of powers and the cosmic person), and <strong>3.10.14–29</strong> (creation classifications and living forms).</p><p>The accessible file is a reflowed e-book, not a scan retaining original printed pagination. Relevant positions in that supplied PDF are pp. 389–390, 435–436, 440–441 and 472–474. Chapter and verse are the primary locators. The Sanskrit text and accompanying explanation supply the reference trail for the original English exposition.</p></section><section id="creation-primary"><h3>Unmanifest nature and primary creation</h3><ul><li><em>The Brahmanda Purana</em>, Part I, Motilal Banarsidass: I.1.3.8–16, printed pp. 28–29 (supplied PDF pp. 36–37), on unmanifest Prakriti/Pradhana, the gunas and Mahat. I.1.5.53–58, printed pp. 44–45 (PDF pp. 52–53), distinguishes Prakrta and Vaikrta creations.</li><li><em>The Linga-Purana</em>, Part I, Motilal Banarsidass: I.3.1–5, 11–27, printed pp. 8–10 (PDF pp. 14–16), on its Shaiva account of primary creation. <strong>I.3.26</strong> cautions that a gradual explanation need not indicate separate events occurring in sequence. I.5.3–17, printed pp. 16–17 (PDF pp. 22–23), supplies its creation classifications.</li></ul><p>Translator notes flag difficulties in parts of Brahmanda I.1.3.17–28 and I.1.5.59; this introduction does not build a detailed chronology from those passages. The different enumerations are retained as belonging to their respective texts.</p></section><section id="creation-egg"><h3>The cosmic egg</h3><p>Linga I.3.28–39, printed pp. 10–11 (PDF pp. 16–17), describes the constituents forming the cosmic egg and Brahma within it. Compare Brahmanda I.1.3.29–38, printed pp. 31–32 (PDF pp. 39–40), and Bhagavata 3.6.1–6 (supplied reflow-file pp. 440–441). The golden egg painting is a conceptual interpretation, not an exact illustration of all these accounts at once. The landscape painting represents differentiated life; its scenery is imagined.</p></section><section id="creation-cycles"><h3>Kalpas, renewal and Pratisarga</h3><p>Bhagavata <strong>3.10.7, 29</strong> concerns renewal after an earlier dissolution and creation at the beginnings of kalpas; <strong>3.11.22–31</strong> describes Brahma’s day/night and the withdrawal of the three worlds (supplied reflow-file pp. 470, 474, 480–481). This is not described here as the destruction of every level of existence.</p><p>Linga I.4.1–6, 36–37, printed pp. 12 and 14 (PDF pp. 18 and 20), supports recurring day/night and renewed beings. Brahmanda <strong>I.1.1.37b–39</strong>, printed p. 8 (PDF p. 16), names Sarga and Pratisarga among Puranic subjects, with the supplied translation identifying Pratisarga as dissolution and recreation. It is not equated with Visarga.</p></section><p>Both Motilal Banarsidass editions were supplied by the owner. Their inspected opening pages do not establish a translator/editor attribution; none is inferred solely from a filename. Book scans and source-review images are not distributed by this website.</p></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="fourteen-lokas"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>The Fourteen Lokas</h2><a href="/fourteen-lokas.html">Return to the guide</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>This introduction follows the owner-supplied <em>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran</em>, Gita Press, Volume 1 (Skandhas 1–8), Sanskrit text with accompanying explanation. <a href="https://archive.org/details/srimad-bhagavat-mahapuran-2-volume-set-sanskrit-hindi/Srimad%20Bhagavat%20Mahapuran%20Volume%201%20Sanskrit%20Hindi/mode/2up" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the exact supplied edition</a>. The accessible copy is a 1,617-page reflowed ebook: PDF positions below identify that file and are not original printed page numbers. Chapter and verse are the primary references. The article is original English exposition; private book scans are not distributed.</p><section id="lokas-fourteen"><h3>The fourteen-world enumeration</h3><p><strong>2.5.36–42</strong>, supplied reflow-file pp. 350–351. Verse 36 introduces seven below and seven above within the Virat Purusha contemplation. Verses 38–39 name the upper seven; 40–41 name the lower seven; 42 offers an alternative three-world body arrangement. These correspondences are identified as contemplative, without presenting one arrangement as an exclusive map for all traditions.</p></section><section id="lokas-upper"><h3>The upper seven</h3><p><strong>2.5.38–39</strong>, reflow-file pp. 350–351: Bhuloka (feet), Bhuvarloka (navel), Svarloka (heart), Maharloka (chest), Janaloka (neck), Tapoloka (breasts), Satyaloka (head, Brahma’s dwelling). The Sanskrit names the last Brahmaloka; the accompanying explanation uses Satyaloka. Verse 42's three-world arrangement is distinct. The guide does not invent individual resident groups for each of the seven or equate a named realm with final liberation.</p></section><section id="lokas-lower"><h3>The lower seven</h3><p><strong>5.24.7–13</strong>, reflow-file pp. 1077–1079, names Atala, Vitala, Sutala, Talatala, Mahatala, Rasatala and Patala, describes their subterranean setting, uses <em>bila-svarga</em> and describes palaces, gardens, inhabitants and illumination.</p><ul><li><strong>5.24.16</strong>, pp. 1079–1080: Atala and Bala, son of Maya.</li><li><strong>5.24.17</strong>, p. 1080: Vitala, Shiva as Hatakeshvara and Bhavani.</li><li><strong>5.24.18, 27</strong>, pp. 1080–1082: Bali’s Sutala and Narayana at his gate.</li><li><strong>5.24.28</strong>, pp. 1081–1082: Talatala and Maya under Mahadeva’s protection.</li><li><strong>5.24.29</strong>, pp. 1082–1083: Mahatala and many-headed serpents descended from Kadru.</li><li><strong>5.24.30</strong>, pp. 1082–1083: Rasatala, Daityas and Danavas.</li><li><strong>5.24.31</strong>, pp. 1082–1083: Patala, Naga lords led by Vasuki and the light of jewels on their hoods.</li></ul></section><section id="lokas-naraka"><h3>Why the lower seven are not simply Naraka</h3><p>Compare the subterranean paradises of <strong>5.24.7–13</strong> with the separate treatment of Naraka in <strong>5.26.4–7</strong>, supplied reflow-file pp. 1089–1090. The latter describes Yama and consequences of actions and gives a different enumeration of punishment realms. The article does not collapse these into the seven lower worlds.</p></section><p><strong>Illustrations:</strong> both paintings are newly AI-generated conceptual interpretations, used once each. The layered horizons are not a measured chart of fourteen levels; the cavern is not identified as one specific Loka. Architecture, landscape and lighting are artistic choices.</p></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="manus-manvantaras"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>The Manus and Manvantaras</h2><a href="/manus-manvantaras.html">Return to the guide</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>This introduction follows the owner-supplied <em>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran</em>, Gita Press, Volume 1 (Skandhas 1–8), Sanskrit text with accompanying explanation. <a href="https://archive.org/details/srimad-bhagavat-mahapuran-2-volume-set-sanskrit-hindi/Srimad%20Bhagavat%20Mahapuran%20Volume%201%20Sanskrit%20Hindi/mode/2up" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the exact supplied edition</a>. The accessible file has 1,617 reflowed ebook pages. PDF positions below locate that file and are not original printed pagination. The article is original English exposition; book scans are not distributed.</p><section id="manus-definition"><h3>A Manu and his period</h3><p><strong>3.11.23–26</strong>, supplied reflow-file pp. 479–480, places fourteen Manus within Brahma’s day and describes recurring Manu lineages, sages, deities, Indra and divine preservation. <strong>8.14.1–7</strong>, pp. 1533–1534, describes appointment and duties. “Cosmic administration” is an editorial explanatory phrase for those coordinated sacred responsibilities.</p></section><section id="manus-responsibilities"><h3>The duties within a Manvantara</h3><p><strong>8.14.2–7</strong>, pp. 1533–1534: divine appointment (2); guidance by divine manifestations and the maintenance of order (3); the seven rishis restoring Vedic dharma (4); each Manu upholding dharma (5); descendants protecting people and deities receiving sacrificial shares (6); Indra sustaining the three worlds and providing rain (7). <strong>8.13.11–12</strong>, pp. 1529–1530, identifies the coming Savarni period and Bali as its Indra. These are the supplied text’s theological descriptions.</p></section><section id="manus-time"><h3>Duration and scale</h3><p><strong>3.11.18–21</strong>, reflow-file p. 479, concerns the four-yuga cycle and its timing qualifications. <strong>3.11.22–24</strong>, pp. 479–480, describes Brahma’s day as one thousand such cycles, the equally long night, and fourteen Manus within the day. Verse 24 uses <em>sadhika</em> for more than seventy-one four-yuga cycles; the accompanying explanation prints seventy-one plus six-fourteenths. The page retains “a little more than seventy-one” rather than silently reducing that to exactly seventy-one.</p><p><strong>8.13.36</strong>, p. 1532, places the fourteen past, present and future Manvantaras within the thousand-four-yuga kalpa. The introduction does not import a separate interval calculation or convert this chronology into a modern calendar date.</p></section><section id="manus-sequence"><h3>The fourteen names in this account</h3><ol><li><strong>Svayambhuva:</strong> 8.1.4, reflow-file p. 1425.</li><li><strong>Svarocisha:</strong> 8.1.19, pp. 1428–1429.</li><li><strong>Uttama:</strong> 8.1.23, p. 1429.</li><li><strong>Tamasa:</strong> 8.1.27, pp. 1429–1430.</li><li><strong>Raivata:</strong> 8.5.2, p. 1453.</li><li><strong>Cakshusha:</strong> 8.5.7, p. 1454.</li><li><strong>Vaivasvata / Shraddhadeva:</strong> 8.13.1, p. 1528.</li><li><strong>Savarni:</strong> 8.13.11, pp. 1529–1530.</li><li><strong>Daksha-savarni:</strong> 8.13.18, pp. 1530–1531.</li><li><strong>Brahma-savarni:</strong> 8.13.21, pp. 1530–1531.</li><li><strong>Dharma-savarni:</strong> 8.13.24, pp. 1530–1531.</li><li><strong>Rudra-savarni:</strong> 8.13.27, p. 1531.</li><li><strong>Deva-savarni:</strong> 8.13.30, pp. 1531–1532.</li><li><strong>Indra-savarni:</strong> 8.13.33, pp. 1531–1532.</li></ol><p>This is the Bhagavata’s list. Alternative names from other texts have not been silently substituted.</p></section><section id="manus-present"><h3>The present seventh Manu</h3><p><strong>8.13.1</strong>, reflow-file p. 1528, identifies Shraddhadeva, son of Vivasvan, as the present seventh Manu, Vaivasvata. The chapter then turns toward the future seven. “Present” is used within this traditional chronology.</p></section><p><strong>Illustrations:</strong> two newly AI-generated conceptual paintings, used once each. The star trails evoke the passage of time without measuring a Manvantara. The cultivated valley evokes sustaining a world; the empty pavilion and landscape are imagined, with no specific Manu, archaeological building or scriptural scene claimed.</p></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="festival-directory-sources"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Expanded sacred calendar</h2><a href="/festivals.html#festival-directory">Return to the directory</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>The added entries draw only on the owner-supplied English Skanda Purana, Linga Purana and Brahmanda Purana scans published by Motilal Banarsidass. Chapter/verse references, printed page numbers and supplied PDF positions below identify the inspected passages. Translator/editor roles are not inferred from filenames. Private scans are not distributed.</p><p>Skanda Part 5 supplies a Purushottama/Jagannath temple-cycle account. Part 6 supplies the Karttika account, and Part 7 supplies the Vaishakha context for Akshaya Tritiya. The accounts are kept distinct, with festivals, temple celebrations, vratas and a devotional season labeled accordingly. The Part 6 calendar uses Asvina dark-half dates before Karttika bright-half dates; that convention is not silently replaced with a universal modern schedule.</p><p>The descriptions are original summaries. Statements of spiritual merit are understood as beliefs expressed by the source; this directory makes no promises of specific outcomes and does not prescribe detailed fasting or ritual regimens.</p>{% for festival in festivals %}{% if festival.reference %}<section id="festival-{{ festival.id }}"><h3>{{ festival.title }}</h3><p>{{ festival.reference }}</p>{% if festival.note %}<p>{{ festival.note }}</p>{% endif %}<p><a href="/festivals.html#calendar-{{ festival.id }}">Return to this entry →</a></p></section>{% endif %}{% endfor %}</div></section><section class="reference-section" id="avadhuta-teachers"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>The Twenty-Four Teachers of the Avadhuta</h2><a href="/avadhuta-teachers.html">Return to the guide</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>This original English guide follows the owner-supplied <em>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran</em>, Gita Press, Volume 2, Sanskrit text with Hindi explanation. <a href="https://archive.org/details/srimad-bhagavat-mahapuran-2-volume-set-sanskrit-hindi" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the exact two-volume supplied edition</a>. Chapter and verse are the primary references; PDF positions below identify the supplied reflowed Volume 2 file, not printed page numbers. Source scans and OCR are not published by this website.</p><section id="avadhuta-frame"><h3>Yadu’s question and the numbered list</h3><p><strong>11.7.19–36</strong>, supplied Volume 2 reflow-file pp. 1101–1107. Krishna recounts King Yadu’s encounter with a wandering Avadhuta. <strong>11.7.33–35</strong> enumerates exactly twenty-four teachers. The Sanskrit passage calls him an Avadhuta or wandering Brahmin; the accompanying Gita Press Hindi explanation identifies him as Dattatreya. The guide preserves that distinction.</p></section><section id="avadhuta-first-eight"><h3>Teachers 1–8: earth to pigeon</h3><p><strong>11.7.37–74</strong>, reflow-file pp. 1107–1114, explains earth, air, sky, water, fire, moon, sun and pigeon. The pigeon is a longer hunter’s-net narrative, and its warning about attachment belongs to this renunciant discourse.</p></section><section id="avadhuta-middle-nine"><h3>Teachers 9–17: python to Pingala</h3><p><strong>11.8.1–44</strong>, reflow-file pp. 1115–1123, explains python, ocean, moth, bee, elephant, honey gatherer, deer, fish and Pingala. The elephant and deer examples are presented within a male ascetic account of sensory restraint. Pingala’s own extended speech and changed outlook are retained in the summary; she is not reduced to a generic symbol.</p></section><section id="avadhuta-final-seven"><h3>Teachers 18–24: kurara to wasp</h3><p><strong>11.9.1–23</strong>, reflow-file pp. 1124–1129, explains the kurara bird, child, maiden, arrow maker, snake, spider and wasp with captive insect. The arrow maker’s concentration is the basis of the imagined illustration. The spider’s web is a theological analogy for manifestation and withdrawal; the wasp image is an analogy about attention, not a claim of zoological mechanism.</p></section><section id="avadhuta-body"><h3>The body after the numbered list</h3><p><strong>11.9.24–25</strong>, reflow-file pp. 1129–1130, presents the body as a further occasion for discernment after the twenty-four are complete. It is not counted as a twenty-fifth member of the numbered list.</p></section><p><strong>Illustrations:</strong> two newly AI-generated conceptual scenes, used once each. The wanderer is unnamed and is not presented as a portrait of Dattatreya; the artisan and distant procession are imagined, not historical depictions. The guide’s closing question is editorial reflection.</p></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="jay-bhole-group"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Jay Bhole Group Ahmedabad</h2><a href="/jay-bhole-group.html">Return to the page</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>The group introduction, founding year and contact details follow the official <a href="https://www.jaybholegroup.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jay Bhole Group website</a>. The portfolio photographs and videos on the page were supplied by the owner and approved for publication. Works shown include Sand Sri Yantra, Chamar, Panch Dhatu Ghanta, Paduka, Ajay Baan and Akhand Bharat. The Sri Yantra reference images are not presented as works. The Jagadguru name and approval description follow the owner’s supplied identification.</p></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="supplied-journal-books"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Supplied books: festivals &amp; sacred places</h2><a href="/places.html">Return to the journal</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>The added textual contexts below were checked against the owner-supplied English Skanda Purana scans published by Motilal Banarsidass. Printed page numbers and PDF page positions identify the supplied copies. These are devotional textual accounts, distinguished from present-day geography, architectural history and other tellings. Book scans are not distributed by this website.</p><ol><li><strong>Shivaratri: Canda’s unintended offering.</strong> Skanda Purana, Part 1, Mahesvara-khanda, Kedara-khanda, chapter 33, especially 9–15 and 29–45. Printed pp. 278–280; supplied PDF pp. 282–284. Chapter begins on printed p. 277. <a href="/festivals.html#shivaratri-telling">Read the short textual context</a>.</li><li><strong>Ramanatha and Ramesvara.</strong> Skanda Purana, Part 8, Brahma-khanda, Setu-mahatmya 43.1–14a. Printed pp. 278–279; supplied PDF pp. 284–285. The footnote identifies Ramanatha and Ramesvara as names of the same linga. <a href="/places.html#setu-text">Read the sacred-place context</a>.</li><li><strong>Kashi’s devotional geography.</strong> Skanda Purana, Part 10, Kashi-khanda, Purvardha 30.69–81. Printed pp. 317–318; supplied PDF pp. 321–322. The footnote calls the Asi/Varana explanation of Varanasi a popular etymology. <a href="/places.html#kashi-text">Read the textual context</a>.</li></ol><p>Browser read-aloud is synthetic speech, with variable pronunciation.</p></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="family">
  <div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Read together · family companion</h2><a href="/family.html">Return to page</a></div>
  <div class="reference-content">
    <p>These original short adaptations retain the chosen accounts’ main events. Discussion questions and everyday applications are editorial invitations, not translated scripture.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/sb/8/7/">Bhagavata Purana · 8.7, Bhaktivedanta Book Trust edition</a><small>Vasuki and Mandara; Kurma’s support; Halahala; Shiva’s compassion; Bhavani’s consent; the blue throat. Key verses: 1, 7–10, 18–20 and 36–43.</small></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/sundara/sarga1/sundararoman1.htm">Valmiki Ramayana · Sundara Kanda, sarga 1</a><small>Hanuman’s crossing, Mainaka, Surasa’s test, Simhika and arrival at Lanka. The crossing is condensed from sarga 1; Rama’s ring and the return report follow sargas 36 and 65, documented under the full Hanuman reader.</small></li>
    </ol>
    <p>The family illustration is an AI-generated devotional interpretation created for its single placement. It is not a historical photograph or a reproduction of a scripture illustration.</p>
