<section class="reference-section" id="dhruva"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Dhruva’s search for the Divine</h2><a href="/dhruva.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p id="ref-dhruva"><strong>Bhagavata Purana · Skandha 4 · chapters 8–9.</strong> This reader uses only the owner-supplied link to <em>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran, Volume 1, Sanskrit–Hindi</em>, Gita Press, Gorakhpur. The scan’s cover identifies it as the illustrated first volume with Hindi explanation. The English narrative is an original condensed retelling, not a reproduction of that Hindi translation.</p><div class="source-list"><a href="https://archive.org/details/srimad-bhagavat-mahapuran-2-volume-set-sanskrit-hindi/Srimad%20Bhagavat%20Mahapuran%20Volume%201%20Sanskrit%20Hindi/mode/2up" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Read the supplied first volume</span><small>Internet Archive · Sanskrit–Hindi scan · Gita Press</small></a></div><ol><li><strong>4.8.7–24</strong><small>Uttanapada’s household, Suruchi’s words, Suniti’s counsel and Dhruva’s departure.</small></li><li><strong>4.8.25–62</strong><small>Narada’s first counsel, Dhruva’s resolve, Madhuvana, the divine form and instruction in worship.</small></li><li><strong>4.8.63–82</strong><small>The king’s remorse, Narada’s reassurance, Dhruva’s tapas and the devas’ appeal.</small></li><li><strong>4.9.1–17</strong><small>Hari’s arrival, the conch’s touch and Dhruva’s prayer.</small></li><li><strong>4.9.18–36</strong><small>The boon of Dhruvaloka, the foretold kingship and Dhruva’s reconsideration of his desire.</small></li><li><strong>4.9.37–59</strong><small>The family reunion and welcome into the city.</small></li></ol><p>The page ends at the homecoming. Extended prayer, detailed austerities, later portions of chapter nine and the subsequent reign are outside its scope. No other edition or Puranic account has been combined with it. The reflection is editorial. All four images commissioned for this reader and its card are AI-generated narrative interpretations; imagined architecture, clothing and landscape are not claims about historical appearance.</p></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="kapila-devahuti"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Kapila and Devahuti</h2><a href="/kapila-devahuti.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Bhagavata Purana · Skandha 3, chapters 24–33.</strong> This original condensed English retelling uses the owner-supplied Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi first volume. Its 1,617-page accessible PDF is reflowed; the PDF positions below are navigational aids, not printed page numbers. The theological and philosophical explanations belong to this Bhagavata account, not to every Sankhya school.</p><div class="source-list"><a href="https://archive.org/details/srimad-bhagavat-mahapuran-2-volume-set-sanskrit-hindi/Srimad%20Bhagavat%20Mahapuran%20Volume%201%20Sanskrit%20Hindi/mode/2up" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Read the supplied first volume</span><small>Internet Archive · Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi edition</small></a></div><ol><li><strong>3.24.10–19, 35–45 · PDF pp. 589–593</strong><small>Brahma identifies Kapila's purpose; Kapila promises to teach his mother; Kardama departs.</small></li><li><strong>3.25.5–20 · PDF pp. 595–598</strong><small>Devahuti's request; mind, attachment, prakriti and the self.</small></li><li><strong>3.25.21–29 · PDF pp. 598–599</strong><small>Compassionate companions, shared sacred stories, devotion and Devahuti's next questions.</small></li><li><strong>3.26.1–8 and following · PDF pp. 603–614</strong><small>Kapila's account of purusha, prakriti and the unfolding principles; the extensive enumeration is condensed.</small></li><li><strong>3.27.1–30 · PDF pp. 615–619</strong><small>Discernment, the dream analogy and freedom from prakriti's binding force.</small></li><li><strong>3.28–29 · PDF pp. 620–637</strong><small>Yoga, Devahuti's further inquiry into bhakti, and conduct toward other beings.</small></li><li><strong>3.33.1–37 · PDF pp. 662–668</strong><small>Devahuti's praise, Kapila's farewell, her practice and the narrator's account of liberation.</small></li></ol><p>The reader omits the detailed yoga discipline, extended catalogue of principles, and chapters 30–32 on embodied suffering and paths after death. It does not combine the Ramayana's Sagara–Kapila episode with this mother–son dialogue. No long translated passage is reproduced. Three new illustrations are AI-generated interpretations: the figures, landscape, lamp and manuscript are imagined rather than documented portraits or sites. The pause-and-reflect question is editorial.</p></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="different-tellings"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Different tellings</h2><a href="/explore.html">Follow a connected journey</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>Read each episode within its own text and theological setting. The comparisons below draw on the narrative references already recorded on this page.</p><details><summary>Kama’s restoration: who responds to Rati?</summary><div class="source-comparison"><article><h3>Shiva Purana</h3><p>In the Parvati Khanda, Shiva responds to Rati’s appeal during the wedding celebrations and restores Kama.</p><a href="#shiva-parvati">Parvati Khanda, chapter 51</a></article><article><h3>Lalita Mahatmya</h3><p>In this Shakta account, Lalita restores Kama after the victory over Bhandasura, responding to Rati’s grief and the devas’ request.</p><a href="#lalita-bhandasura">Lalita Mahatmya, chapter 30</a></article></div></details><details><summary>Halahala: a shared episode, distinct settings</summary><div class="source-comparison"><article><h3>Bhagavata Purana</h3><p>The reader follows the Bhagavata’s Vishnu-centred account: Shiva acts with compassion, Bhavani consents, and the poison leaves a blue mark.</p><a href="#shiva-halahala">Bhagavata 8.7</a></article><article><h3>Skanda Purana</h3><p>The Kedara Khanda foregrounds a Shaiva setting, including Narada’s warning and the neglect of Shiva and Ganesha. These details are not inserted into the Bhagavata retelling.</p><a href="#shiva-halahala">Kedara Khanda, chapters 9–10</a></article></div></details></div></section></div></div><section class="section glossary-addition-sources" id="glossary-additions"><h2>Additional glossary references</h2><p>Short definitions are editorial summaries. The following primary texts provide the contexts used here.</p><ol><li id="glossary-jyotirlinga"><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226440.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Shiva Purana · Kotirudra Samhita 1.20–24</a><small>J. L. Shastri edition; the twelve principal jyotirlingas.</small></li><li id="glossary-itihasa"><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/chandogya-upanishad-english/d/doc239337.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Chandogya Upanishad 7.1.2</a><small>Swami Lokeswarananda edition; itihasa and purana among Narada’s fields of learning. The site’s scripture overview supplies the epic reading context.</small></li><li id="glossary-brahmastra"><a href="https://www.valmikiramayan.net/sundara/sarga48/sundarasans48.htm" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Valmiki Ramayana · Sundara Kanda 48.36–45</a><small>K. M. K. Murthy translation; Indrajit’s use of Brahma’s weapon and Hanuman’s response.</small></li><li id="glossary-linga"><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc225550.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Shiva Purana · Vidyeshvara Samhita 5.1–3</a><small>J. L. Shastri edition; worship of Shiva through the linga and image.</small></li><li id="glossary-avatar"><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/bg/4/6/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Bhagavad Gita 4.6–8</a><small>Bhaktivedanta Book Trust edition; Krishna’s manifestation, read from a Vaishnava translation and commentary.</small></li><li id="glossary-asura"><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/sb/8/7/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Bhagavata Purana 8.7</a><small>Bhaktivedanta Book Trust edition; devas and asuras in the ocean-churning account.</small></li></ol><a href="/glossary.html">Return to the glossary →</a></section></main>

